
title: Na_Start_Day1
/// first time you talk to him after interviewing everyone at the end of day ONE
---
//<<declare $honey_brittle=false>>
//<<declare $russian_classic=false>>
//<<declare $parting_letter=false>>
//<<declare $waddles_portrait=false>>
<<declare $honey_nap_var=false>>
<<declare $naped_dayone_var=false>>
<<declare $nap_letter_var=false>>


Napoleon: *Seated behind a large, ornate desk, its golden details tarnished with age. His head is buried deep in a mountain of papers, his attention absorbed. He barely notices your arrival.*
You: *Clear your throat audibly.*
Napoleon: *Glances up, an eyebrow arched, a faint smirk tugging at his lips.* Ah, detective. Was about to come find you. But, you know.
Napoleon: *He taps a section of papers, each of them adorned with an old photograph of a penguin holding a trumpet.*
You: How are you doing?
Napoleon: *Waves his hand nonchalantly, voice dripping with sarcasm.* es, yes, I am quite well. Interviewing no less than six trumpet penguins an hour—it's a delight. 
Napoleon: Got any news for me?
<<jump NapNewsDayOne>>
===
title: NapNewsDayOne
---
<<declare $nap_wad_pics to false>>
<<declare $nap_waddles_actor to false>>
<<declare $nap_figurine to false>>
<<declare $nap_newspaper to false>>

<<if $nap_wad_pics is false or $nap_figurine is false or $nap_newspaper is false or $naped_dayone_var is false>>
    You: *Open your notebook* Let me see.
<<else>>
    <<jump Nap_End_DayOne>>
<<endif>>
    
    -> You: \[russian classic\]Eduard did visit the victim on the day of the murder..<<if $russian_classic is true and $naped_dayone_var is false>>
        <<jump NapEd_DayOne>>
    -> You: I heard something rather curious about your behavior on the day of the murder. <<if $nap_newspaper is false>>
        <<jump Nap_NewsPaper>>
    -> You: \[figurine\]Is this circus property? I found it in the wagon. <<if $fancy_figurine is true and $nap_figurine is false>>
        <<jump Nap_Figurine>>
    -> You: \[waddles portraits\] Mr. Waddles appears to have an... extensive collection of portraits of himself <<if $waddles_portrait is true and $nap_wad_pics is false>>
        <<jump Nap_WadPics>>
    -> You: I need more time to solve the case. Let us call it a day.
            Napoleon: I see. I suppose expecting this to vanish in a single day was rather too hopeful.*sighs*
            Napoleon: Then I should hope we see each other tomorrow, detective. 
            Napoleon: Keep the key to the wagon, there <i>must</i> be something you are missing.
            You: See you tomorrow, then.
            <<stop>>
        
===
title: NapEd_DayOne
---
Napoleon: Mmhm. Does it prove anything, though? They seem cut from the same old, heavy, dusty fabric.
        You: He was bringing Mr. Waddles a book, it seems. A russian classic.
        Napoleon: That tracks. *Hooks his hands behind his neck, reclining with ease* I am not one to pry into my employees' private affairs...
        Napoleon: But it is difficult not to notice Eduard's fixation with the morbid and the oppressively bleak. He ensures you notice it, in fact.
            -> You: Bad for business, I assume?
                Napoleon: Quite the opposite.
            -> You: I'm somewhat concerned for the young man.
                Napoleon: Oh, don't be. His skin is thicker than the covers of the books he wastes his salary on.
                Napoleon: He revels in every minute of his so-called endless suffering. And, I must admit...
        Napoleon: People love his mannerisms. 
        Napoleon: I happily let him collect misfortune the way others collect trinkets. So long as he hauls himself onto that stage and does <i>what he does best</i>, I don't particularly care.
            -> Acrobat?
                Napoleon: Dear god, no. Can you imagine that?
            -> Joggling?
                Napoleon: Dear god, no. Can you imagine that?
            -> Magic tricks?
                Napoleon: Dear god, no. Can you imagine that?
            -> Nothing?
                Napoleon: Precisely.
        Napoleon: He mostly does nothing but looking miserable. And for some reason, that alone charms the audience.
        Napoleon: *Taps his fingers absentmindedly against a photograph of a penguin holding a trumpet* Misery likes company, detective.
        Napoleon: *A pause* Is there anything else?
        <<set $naped_dayone_var to true>>
        <<jump NapNewsDayOne>>



===
title: Nap_NewsPaper
---
<<set $nap_newspaper to true>>

Napoleon: *looks tired* Can not wait to hear it.
    You: First, how do you think Mr. Waddles felt about the circus?
        Napoleon: He loved playing his trumpet. I paid him handsomely to play his trumpet. Nothing could stop him from playing his trumpet.
        Napoleon: Dream life, if you ask me.
        -> You: Did you have any reason to believe he might have wanted to leave the circus?
        -> You: I could not help but notice that the résumés arrived with remarkable speed. 
        Napoleon: Ah. *leans back* I know where this is going.
        Napoleon: I presume you’ve been informed of the trumpet penguin open-call I placed to the local press.
        Napoleon: I have taken to issuing routine calls for trumpet penguins, as they possess a most unfortunate tendency to... disappear.
        Napoleon: Though, I must confess, this is the first instance in which one has done so by way of murder.
        Napoleon: *Looks at you expectantly*
        -> You: \[letter\] I must warn you... This does not reflect favorably upon you. <<if $parting_letter is true>>
            <<jump NapLetter>>
        -> You: This suffice. We can move on.
            <<jump NapNewsDayOne>>
===

title: NapLetter
---
    Napoleon: Must be good, then.
    -> You: *Say nothing. Throw the resignation letter on his desk making sure to emphasize the gesture dramatically*
    Napoleon: *Looks at the letter, then at your face with amusement* Couldn't wait to do that, huh?
    Napoleon: *Napoleon carefully examines the letter, his eyebrows rising with each line.*
    Napoleon: Good heavens. And I thought Eduard was the dramatic one.
    Napoleon: *Pauses, sighing* I confess, he doesn't sound terribly happy here.
    -> You: Does that surprise you?
    Napoleon: My dear detective, there are two entire paragraphs comparing my refusal to offer five-course gourmet meal as a dinner option every night to using a medieval torture device...
    Napoleon: Am I surprised? No, it is as I expected. Classic Waddles. Did I think this might cause him to leave the circus? Admittedly, no.
    Napoleon: I've never had anyone formally resign. They tend to disappear rather than confront me directly.
        -> You: Pardon?
            Napoleon: *is not listening*
        -> You: *don't interrupt him*
    Napoleon:  I wouldn't have even known how to respond, had I known. He was likely hoping I would beg him to reconsider.
    You: *Curiously* Would you?
    Napoleon: Of course. Anything to avoid the infernal pile of resumes. *Gestures to the disorganized pile of trumpet penguin applications on his desk.*
    You: And if he had not listened... would you have become angry enough to...
    Napoleon: *Fixes you with a steady gaze, his eyes narrowing with an ominous look* choose your next words very carefully, detective.
        -> You: *Pause, then speak slowly* Never mind. For now.
        -> You: To resort to violence?
    Napoleon: *Looks aggrieved* Very well. No sense in delaying the inevitable.
    Napoleon: It was about time to speak plainly. I could see that infernal detective gleam in your eyes, and I find it insufferable.
    Napoleon: *He entwines his fingers and rests his chin upon them, speaking with the deliberate slowness of one addressing a particularly obtuse child.*
    Napoleon: Fine. Let us imagine that I killed him. Then left the letter at the scene, and then I, in an act of sheer lunacy, personally hired the first detective I found to solve the case.
    Napoleon: I can only hope to be more cunning with my next crime.
    Napoleon: *he leans back and folds his arms*
        -> You: Every murderer must begin somewhere. *smile stupidly*
            Napoleon: Perhaps you should grab a clown costume on your way out.*Stares*
        -> You: I was merely asking. No need for such defensiveness.
            Napoleon: *Stares, then sighs* You are correct. You are, after all, merely doing your job.
    Napoleon: I should not presume myself above suspicion, of course.
    Napoleon: It's clear that I am reveling in boundless delight now that he is dead. *Gestures at the mountain of papers upon his desk with an expression of undisguised loathing.*
    Napoleon: Shall we proceed, or have you yet more evidence against me to lay at my feet? *looks more amused than angry*
                <<set $nap_letter_var to true>>
                <<jump NapNewsDayOne>>

                   
===
title: Nap_WadPics
---
    Napoleon: Does he now?
        You: They really have not caught your eyes?
        Napoleon: *raises an eyebrow* Oh, those were portraits of Waddles <i>himself</i>?
        Napoleon: Good God. I thought they were his family.
        Napoleon: I was even-dare I say-a touch moved by what I assumed was an exceptional level of filial devotion.
        Napoleon: *A pause* What an interesting man the world has lost.
        Napoleon: What else do you have?
        <<set $nap_wad_pics to true>>
        <<jump NapNewsDayOne>>                 
===
title: Nap_Figurine
---

Napoleon: *reaches and examines the figurine* Not familiar to me. Must have been his.
    -> You: Looks quite expensive and fine in details.
        Napoleon: *Sets the figurine aside* Emphasis on <i>looks.</i>
    -> You:  Probably had sentimental value.
        Napoleon: i doubt it.
        <<if $nap_wad_pics is true>>
        Napoleon: The man carried a suitcase full of his own portraits. I can’t imagine him appreciating any visual art that wasn’t a life-sized statue of himself.
        Napoleon: Double the size, come to think of it.
        <<endif>>
Napoleon: See, spend enough time away from home, and you start clawing at anything that <i>resembles</i> meaning.
Napoleon: Waddles found meaning in pretending to be someone else. Trinkets like these…
Napoleon: They exude an air of wealth, of worldliness. One places them on a shelf and weaves a story—a life they might have lived.
Napoleon: A life worth returning to.
-> You: … That turned darker than I anticipated.
-> You: And your crew do not have such a life?
Napoleon: *leans forward* There’s something about donning clown attire and joining a travelling circus that doesn’t exactly scream "fulfilling existence."
Napoleon: We are all running from something, my dear detective.
You: And you? What are you running from?
Napoleon:  *slight hesitation*. My taxes. Only to be met with road tolls, fairground fees, and an ungodly amount of paperwork
You: That’s not what you were going to say…
Napoleon: I did have a wittier response in mind. But alas, it’s a circus with a dead body. I fear jokes are still on hold.
Napoleon: *taps on the pile of paper* is there anything else?
<<set $nap_figurine to true>>
<<jump NapNewsDayOne>>
===

title: Nap_End_DayOne
---
You: Nothing else to report.
Napoleon: I see. I suppose expecting this to vanish in a single day was rather too hopeful.*sighs*
Napoleon: Then I should hope we see each other tomorrow, detective. 
Napoleon: Keep the key to the wagon, there <i>must</i> be something you are missing.
You: See you tomorrow, then.
===